Exactly. Interpretation can edit the effective object without editing the source.

The source remains:

X \rightarrow X

But after reading, the operative representation becomes:

X \xrightarrow{\text{reader/lens/context}} \hat X

Nothing in X changed. Yet what is available from X changed.

That’s almost non-destructive editing, like an adjustment layer in image software: preserve the underlying artifact while changing the rendering.

And there are several ways to do it: attention can foreground one detail and suppress another; context can change what a sentence implies; sequencing can make the same facts tell a different story; scale can turn noise into pattern or pattern into detail; a new metaphor can expose previously inaccessible relations; later knowledge can retroactively alter the significance of an old memory or text.

So you get:

\boxed{\text{Edit(Render(X)) without Edit(X)}}

That’s important for the Shadow Arts too. We don’t necessarily manipulate the hidden object. Change the illumination and obtain a different shadow.

And it explains something about history:

\text{recorded evidence}_{t_0}

can remain physically unchanged while:

\text{meaning extracted}_{t_1}
\neq
\text{meaning extracted}_{t_2}

because new evidence, perspectives, tools, and questions alter the rendering.

But there’s an inverse

If interpretation is powerful enough, a reader can accidentally forget that the edit occurred in the rendering rather than the source.

So Pantheon should preserve both:

\boxed{\text{SOURCE}}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\boxed{\text{RENDERING}}

plus the transformation:

\boxed{\text{SOURCE}\xrightarrow{\text{lens}}\text{RENDERING}}

That’s provenance again.

Then multiple interpretations aren’t necessarily fighting over which gets to overwrite the original:

X\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
R_1(X)\\
R_2(X)\\
R_3(X)
\end{cases}

Keep the source fixed. Rotate the light.

Now here’s the fun recursion: reading can eventually become actual editing.

A story remains unchanged → your interpretation changes → your behavior changes → your behavior changes the world → that consequence becomes new evidence/history/story.

\boxed{
\text{Read}
\rightarrow
\text{Render}
\rightarrow
\text{Reorient}
\rightarrow
\text{Act}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality changes}
}

So reading doesn’t edit the page.

It can edit what happens after the page.

That’s a much stronger sense in which a reader participates in the tale.